	<h1>Lineage</h1>
	<p class="subtitle">The Kundeling Tatsak Jedrung Hutoktu Nomun Khan</p>
	<div class="header-rule"></div>
</header>

<section class="lineage-intro fade-in">
	<p>His Eminence the Kundeling Tatsak Jedrung Hutoktu Nomun Khan represents a revered lineage of Lamas, distinguished for their profound influence and storied legacies. Widely celebrated across Tibet, India, China, and Mongolia, they are known for their pivotal contributions to Buddhism and their dual roles as spiritual leaders and political figures. The lineage of Kundeling Tatsak Rinpoche, spanning from the era of Trichen Baso Choekyi Gyaltsen to the present day, serves as an enduring testament to their universally acclaimed virtuous deeds. Each generation has left an indelible mark characterized by extraordinary accomplishments, unwavering dedication to His Holiness the Dalai Lama, and an unwavering commitment to the dissemination of Buddha Dharma alongside political leadership.</p>
	<p>The great Buddhist Master Baso Choekyi Gyaltsen, who was the first incarnation in the lineage of H.E. the Kundeling Tatsak Jedrung Hutoktu Nomun Khan was born in 1402. Baso Chokyi Gyaltsen remains an eminent figure in Tibetan Buddhist history, revered for his profound teachings, unwavering commitment to the Dharma, and his significant contributions to both spiritual and secular realms.</p>
	<p>The 1st Dalai Lama, Gedun Drup, the 1st Panchen Lama, Khedrub Je, and the 1st Kundeling Tatsak Baso Choekyi Gyaltsen were all principal disciples of Je Tsongkhapa, the founder of the Gelugpa sect. They have significant relationship among them that, When Fifth Gaden Tripa, Je Lodoe Choekyong requested the First Dalai Lama to be the Sixth Ganden Tripa. However, Gendun Drup declined the offer and instead recommended that Baso Choekyi Gyaltsen to take his place praising his enormous contribution to Buddha Dharma and his authoritative learning, realization, wisdom, and fame. Therefore, at the request of Gendun Drup, Choeje Lodoe Choekyong and many others, Baso became the Sixth Ganden Tripa or the Holder of the Ganden Throne. And also, Baso Choekyi Gyaltsen was the younger brother of the 1st Panchen Lama, Khedrup Je from whom Je Baso received many sacred teachings.</p>
	<p>In accordance with a prophecy from Manjushri to Tokden Jampel Gyatso, he gave the secret Ear-Whispered teachings to Baso Choeje alone with a strict instruction to keep the teaching confidential. And Khedrup Je also gave the secret oral transmission to Baso Choeje alone. Henceforth Baso Choeje became a custodian of this secret oral transmission. He is also a significant holder in the line of oral transmission of Gedhen Chod emanated from Manjushri, given to him by Tokden Jampel Gyatso. He passed away in 1473. Not only did he leave a great legacy at that time but later also, throughout his successive reincarnations, served as unparalleled Regent of Tibet twice at different times, recognized the Ninth and Thirteenth H.H. the Dalai Lamas without the need for Golden Urn Method, and played the crucial role of tutor to the Eleventh and Thirteenth Dalai Lamas.</p>
	<p>The influence of His Eminence Kundeling Tatsak Rinpoche's lineage extended way beyond Tibet, as the lineage has even served as Chief Spiritual Advisor to the emperor of China and a king of Mongol at times. And has also held the prestigious position of abbot of the Yonghe Gong (Palace of Eternal Peace, built in 1694) in Beijing. Even today, H. E. 13th Kundeling Tatsak Rinpoche travels wide across the globe giving teachings and promoting ethical values.</p>
	<p>Throughout many centuries, the Lamas within the lineage of Baso Choekyi Gyaltsen/Tatsak Jedrungs Hutoktu have dedicated themselves tirelessly promoting the teachings of Buddha. They have not only served sentient beings but have also demonstrated exceptional leadership in diverse political contexts, earning a place of unwavering faith within the hearts of the people.</p>
